WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:03.909 align:start position:0%
 
the u.s housing market  is booming 5.6 million single-family  homes were sold last year  that's more than at any time since the  2006 housing bubble  according to the national association of  realtors and more and more the buyers  are investors  according to john burns real estate  consulting investors purchasing homes to  rent or flip  currently make up about 20 of all u.s  housing sales  during the pandemic millions of  homeowners have fallen behind on their  mortgages  according to the mortgage bankers  association and rental home investors  are preparing for a potential windfall  some investors are finding homes through  a growing niche known as sale  lease backs that's a transaction where a  property owner  sells a home and then rents it back from  the buyer  are programs like these offering an  opportunity to homeowners  or taking one away  that's where i taught my kids to swim  you know it's just little things  you know my first big major purchase i  ever made my life  i don't really see myself living  anywhere else really wanting to  blane marburger has lived in his home in  denison texas outside of dallas  for nearly 20 years he never expected  he'd now be renting it from a new york  real estate investment company  i kept falling short after the pandemic  ravaged his finances  he found a company through an internet  search that offered to buy his house  for a hundred and eighty thousand  dollars and rent it back to him for 1600  a month  with the opportunity to repurchase it  within a year or two  and eventually this was this was my  option  he raised his three kids here and  imagined he'd stay in the house until he  retired from his job as a physical  therapist  then the pandemic hit he says he lost  half his income as he could no longer  make home health visits  i got a forbearance on the house so i  basically had a year that i didn't make  a payment  during the same period he also went  through a divorce and started paying  child support  i think honestly if the pandemic didn't  come  i could have made it marburger says he  fell deeper into debt and his credit  score plunged  even though he had temporary forbearance  protection which allowed him to skip  payments for up to a year and make them  up later  he worried he could lose the home i was  very apprehensive about doing it  but it was kind of i was coming to the  end of my options deciding what am i  going to do  the company marburger found easy knock  was originally conceived to allow  homeowners to start cashing out their  homes when the market was hot or when an  expense arose  but during the pandemic easynox says it  turned its focus on distressed  homeowners who needed access to quick  cash to pay off debt  last summer it raised 25 million dollars  from venture investors  adding to the hundreds of millions of  debt it had already raised according to  crunch base  in texas alone it's purchased more than  100 homes over the past two years  according to public records we've  grown in the triple digits in percentage  terms over the last year  we've bought north of 220 million  dollars worth of homes  and our average home is pretty much in  line with the national average  which is around 280 thousand dollars  easy knock has a new path to financial  freedom  ceo jared kessler says the company  decided to focus on sale lease backs  to offer homeowners with bad credit a  way to take cash out of their house  all you have to pay is monthly if people  wonder if we're the lost and found and  we're the  destination of last resort the answer is  absolutely not  people can sell their home that's an  easy solution for people if you want to  stay in your home  you want the cash out and you want to be  treated with respect  we're the best choice for marburger  the sale lease back was both a last  resort and a lifeline to pay off his  growing debt  easy knock paid 182 thousand dollars for  marburger's house  which it valued at 315 000  that paid off his mortgage balance of  119 000  including a roughly 58 000 lien from  years earlier  and gave him 30 000 cash to pay off his  debt  easy noc took a seven thousand eight  hundred dollar processing fee and is  collecting sixteen hundred dollars in  monthly rent  in twelve to sixteen months marberger  has the option to buy back the house for  a hundred and eighty seven thousand  dollars  the house was about uh four years away  from being totally paid off  before the pandemic so it's like  i just needed four more years man and  but you know it doesn't always work out  the way that you want it to  millions of americans are in a similar  situation as marburger  about 2.2 million homeowners are  currently in forbearance according to  the new york federal reserve bank  serious mortgage delinquencies those  more than 90 days late have quintupled  since the start of the pandemic  according to mortgage data provider  black knight currently  federal and local pandemic restrictions  on foreclosures are protecting some  homeowners from being kicked out  but ira reingold with the national  association of consumer advocates  is worried about what happens when those  protections run out  he remembers seeing programs like sale  lease backs during the housing crisis  as someone who's done consumer  protection for a long time where people  are harmed the most  is when they're desperate when they're  scared and they're desperate  and they're afraid of losing their home  losing  something right they're afraid of going  into bankruptcy  reingold says he understands why sale  leasebacks are appealing for struggling  homeowners  but he thinks they're giving up too much  for quick cash and he says in his  experience  rarely can distressed homeowners buy  their houses back  i understand the short-term benefit of  peace of mind  the short-term benefit of not worried  about being homeless in a year  but you're giving up your long-term  stability  you're giving up all of the equity that  you may have earned in your home  over these years and if you want  generational wealth if it's something  that you want your kids to have someday  or if it's a place that you want to live  for the next 30 years you're giving all  of that up and you're selling it  really low kessler says that at this  point  about a quarter of easy knock customers  have bought back their homes  morgan stanley analysts say they expect  sale lease backs to continue to grow  because of the robust single family  rental market especially for retirees  looking to tap into their home equity  they estimate there could be a million  homes sold through sale lease backs  in the next five to ten years invitation  homes the country's largest home rental  company  says it is planning a sale lease back  program in the fall as another channel  to add to its 80 000 homes  though it's in very early stages  there's no street lights it's just  holes and you know unless you're really  kind of looking for it you'll  drive by it and not even think twice  at this point marberger says he has no  regrets  if you don't pay rent they're gonna come  take your house it's not a non-profit  organization  they're investors they've got to make  their money too  just like all of us do but he's still  optimistic that he'll be able to buy  back his house  at the end of his 16-month contract i  guess honestly the best way to put it is  i'm still hanging on to a lot of  memories i'm not really ready to give  him up yet i'm not really  start that chapter  you

